 will have far more  reliable  information uh compared to others and  some of them may even be useful  but very few of them actually will be  used but  it's going to be a continuum of  information that is very reliable all  the way to completely unreliable  information that is useful all the way  to being completely useless or even  harmful  so the the question is uh not to lament  about the lack of perfection or or the  fact that we have  humanity our human nature deal with with  these problems but just to try to do our  best  and i think that we have ways that we  can optimize the efficiency we can  optimize  the chances that what we will get  will be more reliable and eventually  also more useful  incentives and we're going to we'll come  back to those practical uh  maybe even useful uh uh tips a little  later  harry can i look back to you on on where  we began this little journey  can you imagine a truly objective  science  is that even something which it's worth  trying to bring to mind  or or is it is the enterprise fatally  flawed  from the outset well i mean i can  imagine  it i mean but i can imagine it being  described by a science fiction writer  um and it would you know what would that  world what would that world be like  i i should imagine i i can imagine isaac  asimov writing this  story about it you know and it would the  science would be done by computers  right so would there be humans in the  story i guess is the key question right  well would there be humans in the story  well yeah i can imagine it because i  think there are  sub domains within science where  you can approximate to  the model um claudia has  has been kind of intimating that high  energy physics  might be one of those subdomains but i  don't think she's right because uh  i mean high energy physics is very  interesting  because high energy physics has changed  its standards  for what counts as a discovery in the  1960s  it was through standard deviations and  as she says  nowadays 